.\" tuatara(1): the parts between @ signs are made by frontends/tui/man-pages.lisp.
.TH TUATARA 1 "" "Tuatara @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.ad l
.SH NAME
tuatara \- edit text in the terminal or in a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
@SYNOPSIS@
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tuatara
is a text editor.
It opens each
.I FILE
named, in the terminal it is started in, or in a window of its own with
.B \-\-gui
or when it is started from a launcher, with no terminal, on a desktop.
Without a file it starts in the scratch buffers, where text can be written
and tried without making a file.
.PP
It reads the init file when it starts, and it starts at once: the program is
one executable.
Everything it does is done inside it and documented there: \fBC\-h\fR shows
the help commands, and the handbooks teach each area.
This page covers what is done from the terminal.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-daemon ,
one editor runs in the background, with no screen, and
.B tuatara \-\-client
shows it, a frame of its own, in the terminal it is run in; the daemon's
buffers stay when a client closes, for the next client.
Nothing starts the daemon unless you do: from a shell, or from the desktop as
you log in, with the entry
.B \-\-generate\-autostart
writes.
.B M\-x daemon\-quit
or
.B tuatara \-\-stop
stops it, asking about the changes not saved; sent SIGTERM, as at logout, it
asks nothing, keeps the changes it may as auto-saved copies (never an
encrypted file's text, nor a file opened safely), and ends.
A daemon on another machine is shown the same way,
.BR "tuatara \-\-client=HOST:" ,
its socket brought here by the system's ssh; the files, programs and
everything else stay on that machine, and nothing of this one's clipboard,
agents or terminal goes there, unless the machine is trusted with it
.RB ( "M\-x server\-list" ):
the ssh agent, each use of a key confirmed here; gpg-agent; the clipboard;
files dropped, sent to a directory there.
Plain
.B tuatara
never joins a daemon: case work kept in an editor of its own ends with it.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may stand anywhere before
.BR \-\- ,
among the files.
@OPTIONS@
.PP
An option that is also the name of a file in the directory tuatara is started in
is refused, so that no file there can choose the init file or what is done:
write
.I ./NAME
for the file, or
.B \-\-
before the files.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
With
.BR \-\-merge ,
the merged file was not saved or conflicts are left in it.
With
.BR \-\-generate\-config
or
.BR \-\-generate\-autostart ,
no file was written.
With
.B \-\-client
or
.BR \-\-stop ,
no daemon of that name runs, it ended, or it went on (a change it could not
keep), as is said; with a host, also when ssh cannot reach it, or the Tuatara
there is missing or another build.
When git started tuatara to write a message (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
\fBC\-c C\-k\fR gives the message up and ends the session with 1, so that git
makes no commit; \fBC\-c C\-c\fR saves it and ends the session with 0.
.TP
.B 2
The command line cannot be used: an option or argument that is wrong, a file
that
.B \-\-diff
or
.B \-\-merge
names but is not there, an init file that
.B \-c
names but is not there, standard input that is the terminal for
.BR \- ,
or no terminal to read keys from (use
.BR \-\-gui ).
The message says which; a usage summary follows an option that is wrong.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
@ENVIRONMENT@
.SH FILES
The directories are those of the variables above.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuatara/init.lisp
The init file, written the first time tuatara starts without one, every
setting in it commented out.
.BR tuatara\-init (5)
describes it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuatara/
Your snippets, templates and logos.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tuatara/
What it remembers: files visited lately, places in them, bookmarks, the
session, trust answers and the auto-saved copies.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tuatara/
The grammars you added and the packages fetched.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/tuatara/
Files made again when they are gone, the debug log (debug.log) and the errors
of the program's own (stderr.log); a daemon's log, daemon\-NAME.log.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tuatara/session\-daemon\-NAME
The session of the daemon NAME, kept apart from an editor's own: its clients'
directories and their windows.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tuatara/
The sockets of the daemons running, NAME.socket, and their locks.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/tuatara\-daemon.desktop
The entry that has the desktop start the daemon as you log in, written by
.BR \-\-generate\-autostart ;
tuatara\-daemon\-NAME.desktop for the daemon NAME.
.PP
.BR tuatara\-init (5)
says which of these a later release still reads and which it may remake.
.SH FIRST KEYS
.TP
.B C\-x C\-c
Quit.
.TP
.B C\-g
Stop what is happening.
.TP
.B C\-h C\-a
An introduction to the editor and the ways in to learn it.
.TP
.B C\-h k
Name the command a key runs and say what it does.
.TP
.B M\-x
Run a command by name; M\-x tuatara\-tutor, or
.B tuatara \-\-tutor
from the terminal, teaches the modal keys by editing a text with them.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open two files, the first with the cursor on line 120, column 8:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tuatara +120:8 main.lisp notes.org
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read what another program prints:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git log \-\-stat | tuatara \-
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look at a file that is not trusted, changing nothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tuatara \-\-safe evidence/disk.img
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show the differences of two files when git is asked for a difftool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git config \-\-global diff.tool tuatara
git config \-\-global difftool.tuatara.cmd \e
  \(aqtuatara \-\-diff "$LOCAL" "$REMOTE"\(aq
.fi
.RE
.PP
Merge three versions of a file when git is asked for a mergetool; the exit
status says whether the conflicts were resolved:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git config \-\-global merge.tool tuatara
git config \-\-global mergetool.tuatara.cmd \e
  \(aqtuatara \-\-merge "$LOCAL" "$BASE" "$REMOTE" "$MERGED"\(aq
git config \-\-global mergetool.tuatara.trustExitCode true
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write commit messages in tuatara:
.PP
.RS
.nf
git config \-\-global core.editor tuatara
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run one editor in the background, started by the desktop from now on, and
show it in this terminal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tuatara \-\-daemon
tuatara \-\-generate\-autostart
tuatara \-\-client notes.org
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tuatara\-init (5)
.PP
The handbooks teach each area of the editor; they are the files
tuatara\-NAME\-handbook in the documentation that comes with the release.
In the editor,
.B C\-h C\-a
opens the introduction and
.B M\-x man
shows this page.
.SH AUTHOR
Michael A Jones.
